# Gitleaks fingerprint suppressions.
#
# This is this repository's FIRST suppression of any kind (decision, 2026-09-22,
# "Secret scan false positive" — recorded in docs/final-decisions.md). Any future
# suppression requires its own separate decision; adding an entry here is not routine
# maintenance.
#
# Scope, deliberately narrow, per that decision:
#   - Exact fingerprints only, copied verbatim from `gitleaks detect` output — never
#     hand-constructed, never a path glob, never a rule-wide exclusion.
#   - No change to any gitleaks config file, rule set, or entropy threshold anywhere
#     in this repository.
#   - No inline `gitleaks:allow` comments — they cannot affect commits already in
#     history, and would add a second suppression mechanism alongside this file.
#
# Every entry below is the SAME underlying false positive: gitleaks' generic-api-key
# rule flags a module-scope string constant in the accessibility-gate's e2e test
# harness — the name of a `window` property the sign-in click-diagnostics tracer uses
# to carry evidence between the test and the page it drives (see
# packages/e2e/src/signin-click-diagnostics.ts). It is not a credential, API key,
# token, password, or any other secret: it never touches a real credential,
# environment variable, or external call, and its value is dev-only test-harness
# state, never shipped in the application bundle. The rule was triggered by an
# identifier the constant used to be named (a single all-caps word matching the
# rule's own keyword heuristic), not by the value — confirmed because two
# structurally identical `const KEY = "..."` declarations elsewhere in the same file
# family, holding shorter strings, were never flagged. The identifier has since been
# renamed (packages/e2e/src/signin-click-diagnostics.ts, commit cf28b33) so no NEW
# commit should reproduce this pattern; the entries below cover commits already in
# this branch's history, which a rename cannot retroactively change.
#
# Verified 2026-09-22: a local gitleaks 8.24.3 run (the version this repository's CI
# pins) over this branch's full commit range found exactly these 5 findings, all the
# same rule and identical entropy value (3.784942 — the same 21-character string every
# time), and no others.

